
grep: grep stands for Global Regular Expression Print.
====
grep → Basic regex; use \| for OR.
grep -E → Extended regex; use | for OR.


Option	    Meaning	                               Example
-i	        Ignore uppercase/lowercase	           grep -i "error" app.log
-v	        Show non-matching lines	               grep -v "INFO" app.log
-n	        Show line numbers	                     grep -n "ERROR" app.log
-c	        Count matching lines	                 grep -c "ERROR" app.log
-l	        Show filenames with matches	           grep -l "ERROR" *.log
-L	        Show filenames without matches	       grep -L "ERROR" *.log
-w	        Match whole words only	               grep -w "error" app.log
-x	        Match entire line	                     grep -x "INFO" app.log
-o	        Print only matched text	               grep -o "ERROR" app.log
-h	        Hide filenames	                       grep -h "ERROR" *.log
-H	        Always show filenames	                 grep -H "ERROR" app.log
-q	        Quiet mode; check exit status only	   grep -q "ERROR" app.log
-e	        Specify a search pattern	             grep -e "ERROR" app.log
-f	        Read patterns from a file	             grep -f patterns.txt app.log



grep "ERROR" sample.txt  --> to serach word in file


grep -m 5 'ERROR' app.log  --> Stop after a 5 matches


grep -n "ERROR" sample.txt  --> to print line numbers


grep -i "error" sample.txt   --> to search word in file with case insensitive


grep -in "error" sample.txt  --> Searches case-insensitively and displays line numbers.
grep -in word file1 file2    --> to search in multiple files


grep -c "ERROR" sample.txt  --> Count matching lines
grep -ic word filename   --> count no of lines has word regardingless of case


grep -o "ERROR" sample.txt | wc -l   --> To count every occurrence of the word ERROR rather than matching lines
grep -o '[0-9]\+' sample.txt         --> Find all numbers in a file


grep -v "ERROR" sample.txt --> to print lines which don't have word


grep -w "cat" sample.txt  --> Match whole words, This matches cat as a whole word, not catalog or concatenate 


grep -x "cat" sample.txt   --> Match whole lines


grep 'ERROR\|WARNING' file                       --> to search multiple words
grep -E "ERROR|WARNING" filename                 --> to search multiple words
grep -E '(ERROR|WARNING):' app.log               --> to search multiple words
grep -in -e word1 -e word2 -e word3 filename     --> to search multiple words
grep -in -e word1 -e word2 -e word3 file1 file2  --> to search multiple words in multiple files


grep "^2026-10-10" filename         --> Search for text at the beginning of a line


grep "failed$" filename             --> Search for text at the end of a line


grep '^$' file.txt                  --> to find Empty lines


grep -v '^$' file.txt               --> to find non-empty lines


grep -E '^[[:space:]]*$' file.txt    --> to find Lines containing only whitespace


grep "D.tabase" filename            -->  . matches any single character other than a newline.


grep 'ab*c' file.txt                --> Match zero or more repetitions: *


grep '[aeiou]' file.txt             --> Search using character classes
grep '[0-9]' file.txt
grep '[A-Z]' file.txt


grep -E '[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}' filename        --> -E enables extended regular expression syntax. finding time-shaped text such as 12:30:45; it does not validate whether the time is valid.
grep -E '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' app.log     --> Matches IP-shaped text, not necessarily a valid IPv4 address.


grep -E '[0-9]+' filename               --> Match one or more digits
grep -E '[A-Z]+' filename               --> Match one or more capital letters
grep -E "^[0-9]{3}$" file.txt           --> Match a three-digit code
grep -E '[0-9]{2,4}' file.txt           --> Two to four digits


grep -r "ERROR" /var/log     --> Searches recursively but generally does not follow symbolic links encountered inside directories. It can follow a symlink supplied as a command-line directory argument.
grep -R "ERROR" /var/log     --> Searches recursively and follows symbolic links encountered during traversal, including directory symlinks.


grep -r --include="*.log" "ERROR" logs    --> Search only certain file types by filename
grep -r --exclude="*.log" "ERROR" logs    --> exclude certain type of files
grep -r --exclude-dir="archive" "ERROR" logs    --> exclude directory


grep -A 2 "ERROR" grep_test.log   -->  -A: lines After the match
grep -B 2 "ERROR" grep_test.log   -->  -B: lines Before the match
grep -C 2 "ERROR" grep_test.log   -->  -C: lines before and after


grep -F 'app[1].log' file.txt       -->  -F : Search for fixed text. Without -F, the square brackets have a special meaning in regular expressions.
grep -F "192.168.1.10" app.log      -->  Without -F, the periods in an IP address are regex wildcards. With -F, they are literal periods.


grep -f patterns.txt grep_test.log    --> -f : Search for a pattern from a file. 
                                          --> grep reads the patterns from patterns.txt and searches for any of them.
                                          --> This is useful when you need to search many keywords at once.


grep -o "ERROR" grep_test.log            --> Instead of printing the entire matching line, -o prints only the portion that matched.
grep -o "ERROR" grep_test.log | wc -l    --> Counts non-overlapping matches of ERROR. It counts each occurrence, even when multiple occurrences appear on one line.


grep -q "ERROR" grep_test.log      -->  -q suppresses normal output. The exit status tells you whether a match was found:
echo $?

ExitStatus	Meaning
   0	      At least one match found
   1	      No match found
   2	      Error occurred (GNU grep)

if grep -q "ERROR" grep_test.log; then
    echo "Errors found"
else
    echo "No errors found"
fi


grep -H "error" ./*.log    -->  -H option always displays the filename along with matching lines.


grep -l "ERROR" filename   --> Lists files containing matches. Usually more useful with multiple files or recursive searches.


grep -L "ERROR" filename   --> Show filenames without matches, 


tail -f app.log | grep --line-buffered -i 'error'    --> grep processes the output line by line
                                                     --> Each matching line is flushed immediately.
                                                     --> You can see errors as they occur, making real-time monitoring more responsive.


zgrep -i 'ERROR' app.log.gz         --> Search in compressed logs



find . -type f -exec grep -l "search_string" {} +   --> Combine find with grep to search file contents , -l -> Only print the filename, not the matching line.
                          -Hn                        --> -H    → print filename , -n    → print line number
                                                     --> {} represents each file found by find
                                                     --> \; Marks the end of the -exec command

